# 1️⃣ Parameterized Recursion

## 📖 Definition

Parameterized Recursion carries the answer through function parameters instead of returning it.

---

### Example

Find the sum of the first N natural numbers.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void sum(int n, int ans)
{
    if(n == 0)
    {
        cout << ans;
        return;
    }

    sum(n - 1, ans + n);
}

int main()
{
    sum(5, 0);
}
```

---

### Output

```
15
```

---

### Characteristics

✅ Uses extra parameters

✅ No return value required

✅ Builds the answer during recursive calls

---

# 2️⃣ Functional Recursion

## 📖 Definition

Functional Recursion returns the computed result from each recursive call.

---

### Example

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

int sum(int n)
{
    if(n == 0)
        return 0;

    return n + sum(n - 1);
}

int main()
{
    cout << sum(5);
}
```

---

### Output

```
15
```

---

### Characteristics

✅ Returns a value

✅ Cleaner implementation

✅ Easier to combine recursive results

---

# 3️⃣ Multiple Recursion

## 📖 Definition

A recursive function makes more than one recursive call.

---

### Example

Fibonacci Series

```cpp
int fibonacci(int n)
{
    if(n <= 1)
        return n;

    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
}
```

---

### Recursive Tree

```
                 Fib(5)
               /       \
           Fib(4)      Fib(3)
          /     \      /     \
      Fib(3) Fib(2) Fib(2) Fib(1)
```

---

### Time Complexity

```
O(2ⁿ)
```

---

### Space Complexity

```
O(n)
```

---

# 4️⃣ Recursion Tree

## 📖 Definition

A Recursion Tree is a graphical representation of recursive function calls.

---

### Example

Factorial

```
factorial(4)

↓

4 × factorial(3)

↓

3 × factorial(2)

↓

2 × factorial(1)

↓

1
```

---

### Advantages

✅ Helps visualize recursive execution

✅ Makes complexity analysis easier

✅ Useful in Dynamic Programming

---

# 5️⃣ Divide & Conquer

## 📖 Definition

Divide the problem into smaller subproblems, solve each recursively, and combine their results.

---

### Examples

- Merge Sort
- Quick Sort
- Binary Search

---

### Steps

```
Problem

↓

Divide

↓

Solve Smaller Problems

↓

Combine Results
```

---

### Advantages

✅ Efficient for large datasets

✅ Reduces problem complexity

---

# 6️⃣ Tail Recursion Optimization

Some compilers optimize Tail Recursion by converting it into loops.

---

### Example

```cpp
void print(int n)
{
    if(n == 0)
        return;

    cout << n << " ";

    print(n - 1);
}
```

---

### Advantages

- Less stack memory
- Better performance
- Similar to iterative solutions

---

# 7️⃣ Memoization (Introduction)

## 📖 Definition

Memoization stores the results of previously solved recursive subproblems to avoid repeated calculations.

---

### Without Memoization

```
Fib(5)

↓

Fib(4)

↓

Fib(3)

↓

Fib(2)

↓

Fib(1)

Fib(2) again

Fib(3) again
```

Many calculations repeat.

---

### With Memoization

```
Fib(3)

↓

Store Answer

↓

Reuse Whenever Needed
```

---

### Advantages

✅ Eliminates repeated computations

✅ Greatly improves performance

✅ Foundation of Dynamic Programming

---

# 📊 Complexity Comparison

| Concept | Time | Space |
|----------|------|--------|
| Parameterized Recursion | O(n) | O(n) |
| Functional Recursion | O(n) | O(n) |
| Multiple Recursion | O(2ⁿ) | O(n) |
| Divide & Conquer | Depends on Problem | Depends |
| Memoization | O(n) (Problem Dependent) | O(n) |

---

# 💡 Interview Tips

✅ Use Functional Recursion when a value needs to be returned.

✅ Use Parameterized Recursion when passing accumulated results.

✅ Draw a Recursion Tree to understand execution flow.

✅ Avoid repeated recursive calls whenever possible.

✅ Memoization converts many exponential recursive solutions into polynomial time.

---

# ⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Forgetting the Base Case

❌ Infinite Recursion

❌ Stack Overflow

❌ Recomputing the same subproblem

❌ Returning incorrect values

---

# 📝 Summary

- Parameterized Recursion carries the answer as a parameter.
- Functional Recursion returns the answer.
- Multiple Recursion creates multiple recursive branches.
- Recursion Trees help visualize recursive execution.
- Divide & Conquer recursively solves independent subproblems.
- Tail Recursion can be optimized into iteration.
- Memoization avoids repeated calculations and improves efficiency.

